Licence of the shader and of the data file
  The shader file has no licence text of its own, so the licence is the one of the repository it was
  written for. Statements, quoted exactly:

  LICENSE (https://github.com/gke0op/argus/blob/main/LICENSE, lines 1-3):
    "MIT License

    Copyright (c) 2026 Ekmel Ozdemir"
  followed by the standard MIT permission notice, reproduced in full below.

  NOTICE.md (https://github.com/gke0op/argus/blob/main/NOTICE.md, line 3):
    "Argus is released under the MIT License (see `LICENSE`)."

  README.md, section "Licence" (https://github.com/gke0op/argus#licence):
    "MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE). Data files carry their own licences (the Orion Nebula data is
    CC0 and CC BY 4.0); see [NOTICE.md](NOTICE.md)."

  The shader's code credits no other author's shader and contains no third-party source code
  (NOTICE.md, "Third-party data": "The repository contains no third-party source code."). The
  published literature it cites is for physical constants and methods, cited in its header.

Measured data carried in orion-a.png and orion-b.png, and its own licences
  The two PNG files are the two halves (rows 0-491 and rows 492-983) of themes/Orion_Nebula.bin,
  byte for byte. They hold data, not pictures; the alpha channel is data too. The file is the
  author's down-sampled, 8-bit log-encoded extract of the sources below (NOTICE.md, "Third-party
  data"). Each source keeps its own licence:

  1. VLT/MUSE maps of the Orion Nebula: Weilbacher, P. M. et al. 2015, A&A 582, A114, "A MUSE map
     of the central Orion Nebula (M 42)"; data published by the Leibniz-Institut fuer Astrophysik
     Potsdam (AIP), doi:10.17876/data/2023_3, under Creative Commons CC0 1.0 ("Creative Commons CC0
     for data"). Based on observations made with ESO telescopes at the La Silla Paranal Observatory
     under programme ID 60.A-9100(A). The author down-sampled the reddening-corrected line maps (Hbeta
     to Halpha, [O III], [N II], [S II], [O I]) and the extinction map, masked bright stars, and
     encoded them as 8-bit logarithms.
     Licence: CC0 1.0. "To the extent possible under law, the author has waived all copyright and
     related rights to this work." https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
     No credit is required; it is given here anyway.

  2. 3D dust map: Edenhofer, G. et al. 2024, A&A 685, A82, "A parsec-scale Galactic 3D dust map out
     to 1.25 kpc from the Sun"; data doi:10.5281/zenodo.8187943, licensed under CC BY 4.0
     (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Required credit: the attribution above, the
     licence link, and an indication of changes. Changes made by the author of Argus: "we read the
     posterior mean and standard deviation along the 64 HEALPix sightlines around M42 and kept only
     the integrated extinction in front of the Orion complex (one number, `C_FG`), which is subtracted
     from the MUSE extinction map before encoding." That number is the constant C_FG in main.metal
     (0.0393, c(Hbeta) of the diffuse medium in front), and its subtraction is in the Veil channel of
     the data.

  3. Star positions and magnitudes of 12 bright stars: the SIMBAD database, operated at CDS,
     Strasbourg, France (Wenger et al. 2000, A&AS 143, 9). The positions are baked into main.metal.

  The remaining numbers in main.metal (line ratios, recombination coefficients, extinction law,
  stellar temperatures, cluster structure) are published physical constants and parameters, cited in
  the header of main.metal where they are used.

What was changed for this wallpaper
  The shader was ported from Argus's compute-kernel form (argus_init and argus_draw) to one fragment
  function: the state it baked is rebuilt from the two images where it is needed, the per-tile star
  culling became a screen grid, and the constant tables were declared the way the scene wrapper needs.
  The physics, constants, colours, camera and display stretch are the author's. The full list is at
  the top of main.metal. The two PNG files are the author's data file split in two, with no byte
  changed. scene.json sets 0.7 draw scale and 10 frames a second.

The licence of this port (shader, data file and credits as above) is the MIT License below; the data
sources in 1 and 2 keep the licences stated above (CC0 1.0, and CC BY 4.0 with the credit above).

